  ex:l8-script-material-5-9 a cd:ScriptMaterial ; cd:position 9 ; cd:scriptResource ex:l8-media-decay-log ; dct:title "Fit a dimensionless log ratio: figure"@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l8-script-material-5-10 a cd:ScriptMaterial ; cd:position 10 ; cd:scriptResource ex:l8-f-logfit ; dct:title "Fit a dimensionless log ratio: formula and complete symbol legend"@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l8-script-material-5-11 a cd:ScriptMaterial ; cd:position 11 ; cd:scriptResource ex:l8-f-back ; dct:title "Back-transform, but keep the criterion clear: formula and complete symbol legend"@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l8-script-material-5-12 a cd:ScriptMaterial ; cd:position 12 ; cd:scriptResource ex:l8-f-criteria ; dct:title "Which residual scale are we minimising?: formula and complete symbol legend"@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l8-script-material-5-13 a cd:ScriptMaterial ; cd:position 13 ; cd:scriptResource ex:l8-fit-reciprocal-auto ; dct:title "A reciprocal term gives a special hyperbola: formula and complete symbol legend"@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l8-script-material-5-14 a cd:ScriptMaterial ; cd:position 14 ; cd:scriptResource ex:l8-f-reciprocal ; dct:title "A reciprocal term gives a special hyperbola: formula and complete symbol legend"@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l8-script-material-5-15 a cd:ScriptMaterial ; cd:position 15 ; cd:scriptResource ex:l8-media-category-design ; dct:title "Optional bridge: categories also become columns: figure"@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l8-script-material-5-16 a cd:ScriptMaterial ; cd:position 16 ; cd:scriptResource ex:l8-f-anova ; dct:title "Optional bridge: categories also become columns: formula and complete symbol legend"@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l8-script-ch5 a cd:ScriptSection ; cd:position 5 ; dct:title "Applications of known model terms"@en ; cd:body "**Change the terms, keep the coefficient problem**\n\nApplications now use different known terms or a transformed response. Ask what changes in X and what quantity is being minimised. A curved graph can still be linear in coefficients. Log-transforming a response also changes the residual criterion, so it requires a separate explanation.\n\n[[ref:ex:l8-script-material-5-1]]\n**A heating ramp is visibly curved**\n\nThe new synthetic heating data use times 0,1,2,3,4 min and temperatures 20.98,22.34,25.20,29.66,35.82 °C. In the manual quadratic experiment, begin with an intercept of 20 °C, a time coefficient of 2 °C/min and a squared-time coefficient of 0 °C/min². Ask learners what changes as the squared-time coefficient moves. Compare curves with the same fixed observations and keep the automatic fitted coefficients for the later result scene.\n\n[[ref:ex:l8-script-material-5-2]]\n**Build the columns [1, x, x squared]**\n\nThe design now has five rows and three columns. The third column is 0,1,4,9,16 min², computed from the measured times before fitting. Coefficients are ordered constant, time, squared time. The temperature model is beta0 plus beta1 times x plus beta2 times x squared. Squaring the known predictor does not square an unknown coefficient.\n\n[[ref:ex:l8-script-material-5-3]]\n**Fit the quadratic ramp**\n\nThe quadratic least-squares coefficients are 21 °C, 0.5 °C/min and 0.8 °C/min². Predictions are 21.00,22.30,25.20,29.70,35.80 °C; residuals are −0.02,+0.04,0,−0.04,+0.02 °C and SSE is 0.004 °C². For comparison, the fitted straight line has intercept 19.4 °C and slope 3.7 °C/min with SSE 8.964 °C². Adding a term cannot increase training SSE when the original columns remain available; a smaller training score alone is not a universal model-selection rule.\n\n[[ref:ex:l8-script-material-5-4]]\n\n[[ref:ex:l8-script-material-5-5]]\n**Linear refers to the coefficients**\n\nEach unknown coefficient multiplies one known column and the resulting contributions are added. The quadratic model therefore remains a linear coefficient problem. In contrast, an unknown decay constant inside an exponential cannot be treated as a fixed design term on the raw response scale. This definition is about the original specified coefficient representation, not about whether a graph looks straight.\n\n[[ref:ex:l8-script-material-5-6]]\n**A positive concentration decays**\n\nGenerate concentration as 20 mg/L times exp(−0.4 per min times t plus epsilon), with dimensionless log perturbations [−0.02,+0.04,0,−0.04,+0.02]. These are synthetic positive concentrations, not the drift signals. Retain the unrounded values from the canonical dataset. The figure shows them on the raw concentration scale; a logarithm will make the specified exponential relation a straight-line coefficient problem.\n\n[[ref:ex:l8-script-material-5-7]]\n\n[[ref:ex:l8-script-material-5-8]]\n**Fit a dimensionless log ratio**\n\nDefine z as ln(C/C*) with C* fixed at 1 mg/L, so no logarithm is taken of a dimensional quantity. Fit z on time with an intercept. For these perturbations the signed sum and time-weighted sum are both zero, giving intercept ln(20) = 2.995732273553991 and slope −0.4 per min. The log-scale SSE is 0.004 dimensionless. This is a fit to z, not a direct least-squares fit to C.\n\n[[ref:ex:l8-script-material-5-9]]\n\n[[ref:ex:l8-script-material-5-10]]\n**Back-transform, but keep the criterion clear**\n\nExponentiate the fitted log response and multiply by the reference concentration C* to obtain 20 mg/L times exp(−0.4t). This is the back-transformed fitted log response; it is not automatically an unbiased arithmetic mean concentration. The following scene compares the two possible residual criteria explicitly.\n\n[[ref:ex:l8-script-material-5-11]]\n**Which residual scale are we minimising?**\n\nLog-scale least squares minimises squared differences of dimensionless logarithms. Raw-scale least squares instead minimises squared concentration differences in (mg/L)². The fitted log line therefore need not minimise raw concentration SSE. For the authored decay data its log SSE is 0.004, while the raw SSE at the back-transformed log fit is about 0.518628 (mg/L)². Those scores have different units and cannot be ranked against each other. Choose the criterion for the intended measurement model, rather than choosing whichever number is smaller.\n\n[[ref:ex:l8-script-material-5-12]]\n**A reciprocal term gives a special hyperbola**\n\nFor positive flow q in mL/min, choose known columns [1,1/q] and fit residence time in min. The illustrative relation is 0.4 min plus 8 mL divided by q. At q = 1,2,4,8,16 mL/min the times are 8.4,4.4,2.4,1.4,0.9 min. The coefficient problem is linear in the two coefficients, even though the curve in q is hyperbolic. Zero flow is excluded. This special reciprocal model does not imply that general saturation models are linear.\n\n[[ref:ex:l8-script-material-5-13]]\n\n[[ref:ex:l8-script-material-5-14]]\n**Optional bridge: categories also become columns**\n\nReturn to the existing Level 7 river-water recovery data for methods A, B and C, each with six observations and means 94%,98%,93%. Choose A as baseline and rows [1,0,0], [1,1,0], [1,0,1] for A,B,C. The coefficients are 94%, +4 percentage points and −1 percentage point; fitted values are the group means and SSE is 30 squared percentage points. This is an encoding bridge only: no F statistic, inference or new ANOVA data are introduced.\n\n[[ref:ex:l8-script-material-5-15]]\n\n[[ref:ex:l8-script-material-5-16]]"@en ; cd:hasScriptMaterial ex:l8-script-material-5-1, ex:l8-script-material-5-2, ex:l8-script-material-5-3, ex:l8-script-material-5-4, ex:l8-script-material-5-5, ex:l8-script-material-5-6, ex:l8-script-material-5-7, ex:l8-script-material-5-8, ex:l8-script-material-5-9, ex:l8-script-material-5-10, ex:l8-script-material-5-11, ex:l8-script-material-5-12, ex:l8-script-material-5-13, ex:l8-script-material-5-14, ex:l8-script-material-5-15, ex:l8-script-material-5-16 ; dct:references ex:l8-script-material-5-1, ex:l8-script-material-5-10, ex:l8-script-material-5-11, ex:l8-script-material-5-12, ex:l8-script-material-5-13, ex:l8-script-material-5-14, ex:l8-script-material-5-15, ex:l8-script-material-5-16, ex:l8-script-material-5-2, ex:l8-script-material-5-3, ex:l8-script-material-5-4, ex:l8-script-material-5-5, ex:l8-script-material-5-6, ex:l8-script-material-5-7, ex:l8-script-material-5-8, ex:l8-script-material-5-9 ; cd:relatedScene ex:l8-scene-43, ex:l8-scene-44, ex:l8-scene-45, ex:l8-scene-46, ex:l8-scene-47, ex:l8-scene-48, ex:l8-scene-49, ex:l8-scene-50, ex:l8-scene-51, ex:l8-scene-52, ex:l8-scene-55 ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l8-script-ch6 a cd:ScriptSection ; cd:position 6 ; dct:title "What we know and what remains open"@en ; cd:body "**What stays the same across all examples?**\n\nBuild the known columns, preserve row alignment, fit the coefficients and interpret predictions in the response unit. Straight, quadratic and reciprocal curves differ in known terms; categorical predictors use indicator columns. Log-response fitting changes both the response and the criterion. Full column rank is needed for unique coefficient values. A small training residual score is not the same as a quantified uncertainty or a causal conclusion.\n\n[[ref:ex:l8-script-material-5-1]]\n**How certain is our predicted signal?**\n\nWe have computed a fitted line and an interpolation at 2.5 min, but have not quantified uncertainty in coefficients or future responses. Which quantities might change if we repeated the measurements? Leave the question open for the next appointment: today’s fitted values are not yet a quantified statement about their uncertainty.\n\n[[ref:ex:l8-script-material-1-1]]"@en ; dct:references ex:l8-script-material-1-1, ex:l8-script-material-5-1 ; cd:relatedScene ex:l8-scene-53, ex:l8-scene-54 ; cd:authoredResource true .
